From c6cedd75938010eb42d17293a156c0c473b10a79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?St=C3=A9phane=20Graber?= <stgraber@stgraber.org>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 22:42:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] incusd/auth: Treat unknown certificates as restricted in the
 router
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The authorization router classified any fingerprint it could not find in
the trust store as unrestricted, routing it to the allow driver. A
restricted client could then make its own fingerprint leave the cache
mid-request and reach the server with full rights.

The router now reads the trust store entry's restricted flag and treats
an unknown fingerprint as restricted, unless a CA-trusted certificate is
possible. The TLS driver no longer falls back to trusting any
certificate when server.ca exists.

This addresses GHSA-9m5m-cr24-m367 (CVE pending)

Signed-off-by: Stéphane Graber <stgraber@stgraber.org>
(cherry picked from commit 15de6a7f0589823da5352e31a8f35db41b8e1cae)
